  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nThe coldest airmass so far this season over the area has led to\ntemps struggling to rise out of the mid 40s early this \nafternoon, with 50 degrees looking to be out of reach for a vast\nmajority of the area. With a surface ridge drifting toward the \narea from the west, limited non-diurnal cloud cover upstream, \nand cool daytime temps, we are well on our way to the coldest \nnight and first hard freeze for many this fall. Do have some \nconcern that shallow stratus attempts to expand inland off Lake \nMichigan this evening, but lowering inversion heights through \nthe night should erode any potential lingering stratus \novernight. Overall, temps tonight are expected to fall into the \nmid to upper 20s away from the core of the Chicago metro and \nnorthwest Indiana downwind of Lake Michigan. In colder areas \ntonight, shallow fog may develop in lieu of heavy frost. \nOtherwise, isolated lake effect rain showers will persist into \nmainly northern Porter County through early evening before \nexiting to the east.\n\nA series of weak mid-level troughs and ridges within broader \nWNW flow aloft will cross the northern Great Lakes through early\nnext week, leading to precip-free conditions and daytime highs \ngradually moderating back to the low to mid 60s Monday and \nTuesday.\n\nConsensus ensemble guidance suggests that an increasingly \namplified mid-level pattern with hints at a developing omega \nblock will develop across much of North America late next \nweek/weekend. The eastern trough of this pattern does show signs\nof settling over the Great Lakes region during this period. \nWith the main ridge situated in a SSW to NNE orientation into \nHudson Bay, seasonably cool temps and north to northeast low-\nlevel flow will support periods of lake effect rain showers \naimed into both northwest Indiana and northeast Illinois. Cannot\nfully rule out our first snow flakes of the season given some \nensemble members supporting a more westward presentation of the \ntrough and corresponding colder airmass. Will need to keep a \nclose eye on any trends toward colder solutions as early as \nThursday morning but especially into Friday.\n\nKluber",
